  "live_records": {
    "q-337322": {
      "dom_id": "step-q337322",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter, in terms of $x$ and $y$ only.",
      "worked_solution": "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}) \\\\ 2y{y}^{′} & =\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{C}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4{C}^{2}}{x}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{2}=-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2(-\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4})}{{x}^{2}y}=-\\frac{y}{2x}.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{2x})}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: For the original family, since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ whenever $x\\ne 0,$ hence, the slopes are well-defined. Otherwise, when $x=0,$ the curve is not defined.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
