    "q-336616": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336616",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters.",
      "worked_solution": "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $Cy={e}^{2x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(Cy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({e}^{2x}) \\\\ C{y}^{′} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\nCy={e}^{2x}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y})}=2y.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-1 \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-1)\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =-\\int dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+x & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+x=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14189464/assets/e941d35788f4aee837220962511ca537528b04dec9efaa7f956a4ee571e14189.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined.",
